---
import Media from '../Media.astro';
---

<!-- ============ Recruiter Skim ============ -->
<!-- id stays "ats" — it's a published anchor other pages and posts link to. -->

<section id="ats" data-scroll class="border-t border-line px-5 py-24 md:py-32">
  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl">
    <p
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="ghost-num pointer-events-none absolute -top-4 right-0 -z-10 font-mono text-[7rem] leading-none font-bold tracking-tighter text-ink/[0.04] select-none md:text-[11rem] dark:text-ink/[0.07]"
    >
      02
    </p>
    <p class="tint-eyebrow reveal flex w-fit items-center rounded-lg px-2.5 py-1 font-mono text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.18em]">
      @@ recruiter skim @@
    </p>
    <h2 class="reveal mt-5 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
      See where you actually stand.
    </h2>
    <p class="reveal reveal-2 mt-4 max-w-[65ch] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      No algorithm scores your resume. An applicant tracking system is a database with a
      search box, so what actually ends an application is a requirement you don't meet:
      visa status, a license, a location. Udaan checks those first, then the words a
      recruiter would search for, with a suggested rewrite for each issue it finds.
    </p>

    <div class="reveal reveal-2 mt-10">
      <Media
        name="ats-analysis"
        alt="The Recruiter Skim panel, leading with knockout-risk flags, then a skim verdict, keyword coverage, and title alignment"
        ratio="aspect-[406/249]"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="reveal flex gap-4">
        <span class="tint-icon mt-0.5 shrink-0 font-mono text-sm">01</span>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Knockout risks.</span> Work authorization, a
          required license, a location you can't work from. The things that genuinely end an
          application, flagged on their own rather than buried in a keyword list.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal flex gap-4">
        <span class="tint-icon mt-0.5 shrink-0 font-mono text-sm">02</span>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Keyword coverage.</span> Every requirement in
          the posting checked against your resume and marked as an exact match, a close one,
          or missing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal flex gap-4">
        <span class="tint-icon mt-0.5 shrink-0 font-mono text-sm">03</span>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Title alignment.</span> Whether your most
          recent title reads as the role they posted. Some systems weight job title heavily,
          Workday among them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="reveal mt-12 max-w-[65ch] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      All of that is about the document, and every finding comes with a button that fixes
      it. The harder question sits next to it in <span class="font-semibold text-ink"
        >Fit Check</span
      >: whether you're actually a fit for the job. It reads your resume the way a hiring
      manager would and says plainly where you're short on experience, seniority, or
      evidence. Most of what it finds can't be fixed by editing a line, so it doesn't
      pretend otherwise — a gap that needs a real credential is labelled as one instead of
      being quietly reworded away.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
